<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lebanese Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1975">1975</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lebanese Civil War was fought from 1975 to 1990 among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>'s sectarian militias, Palestinian groups and outside powers. Christian, Muslim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> factions fought over political power, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and Israel invaded and occupied parts of the country. About 120,000 people died, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif-agreement">Taif Agreement</a> of 1989 ended the war by rebalancing power between the communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lebanese-civil-war.jpg" alt="Lebanese Civil War" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Case from Philadelphia, Mississippi, U.S.A. · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green_Line,_Beirut_1982.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> became independent on 22 November 1943, and its political system divided offices by community: the president was to be a Maronite, the prime minister a Sunni Muslim and the speaker of parliament a Shia Muslim. The country was relatively calm in the 1960s. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-of-jordan">King Hussein</a> evicted the Palestinian armed groups from Jordan in the events of 1970 known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-september">Black September</a>, they relocated their fighting strength to Lebanon, where some 400,000 Palestinian refugees already lived in camps. They created what was called a state within the state, which strained Lebanon's fragile sectarian balance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war began on 13 April 1975, when unidentified gunmen fired on a church in Ain el-Rummaneh, a Christian suburb of East Beirut, killing four people, and Phalangists then killed 30 Palestinians travelling in a bus. The two main alliances were the Lebanese Front of nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronites</a> and the pro-Palestinian Lebanese National Movement. Syria moved troops into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> in 1976 at the request of President Suleiman Frangieh. Beirut was divided along the Green Line, with the Palestinian and Muslim militias in the west and the Christians in the east. As central authority broke down, parties set up cantons of their own, such as the Phalangist territory known as Marounistan.</p><p>Israel invaded in March 1978, in Operation Litani, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> created UNIFIL. Israel invaded again in June 1982, and between 16 and 18 September 1982 Phalangists allied with Israel killed civilians at the Sabra and Shatila camps in numbers estimated between 460 and 3,500. A multinational force of US Marines and French, Italian and British units was deployed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> helped to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Bekaa Valley</a> from 1982. Later fighting included the Mountain War of 1983, the War of the Camps from 1985 and the clashes between Amal and Hezbollah in 1988.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In October 1989 Lebanese parliamentarians meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Taif</a>, in Saudi Arabia, agreed a national reconciliation accord, after a committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> had formulated solutions. They ratified it on 4 November and elected René Mouawad as president the next day. General Michel Aoun in East Beirut refused to accept it, and the Lebanese Forces under Samir Geagea clashed with him in 1990. In March 1991 parliament passed an amnesty for political crimes, and in May the militias, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a>, were dissolved.</p><p>The war displaced about 900,000 people, a fifth of the pre-war population, and perhaps a quarter of a million emigrated permanently. Thousands of land mines remained, and the victims of kidnapping and of wartime disappearance number in the tens of thousands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Taif</a> accord gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> a large role in Lebanese affairs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lebanese Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
